# LLM-discoverability HTTP headers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS:
# Per Evil Martians research (April 2026), AI agents that fetch HTTP
# responses without parsing HTML rely on the response `Link` header to
# discover a markdown rendering of a page. Headless tools (Claude Code
# fetcher, Cursor file reader, future ChatGPT browse modes) read the
# header and skip the HTML body entirely. JSON-LD does not reach those
# tools — only the wire-level HTTP signals do.
#
# WHEN THIS FILE ACTIVATES:
# `_headers` is a Cloudflare Pages and Netlify deploy-time configuration
# file. The current production deployment is GitHub Pages with Cloudflare
# DNS in front, and GitHub Pages IGNORES this file. So today the
# per-route `Link` headers below are NOT served by the CDN — only the
# `<link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown">` HTML tag and the visible
# `.md` endpoints are doing real work for LLM discovery.
#
# To activate these headers without leaving GitHub Pages, the cheapest
# path is a Cloudflare Worker / Transform Rule on the existing zone that
# injects the same `Link` header per pathname. See
# `functions/_middleware.ts` in this repo for the equivalent runtime
# implementation if/when the deployment moves to Cloudflare Pages or any
# host that runs edge functions. Vercel reads its own `vercel.json`
# headers config; the same rules can be ported by hand.
#
# All `.md` endpoints used below are emitted at build time by
# `src/pages/[...path].md.ts`.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default headers on every URL. Points headless agents at the studio-wide
# llms.txt + llms-full.txt context maps regardless of which page they hit.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
/*
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="alternate"; type="text/plain"; title="LLM context", </llms-full.txt>; rel="alternate"; type="text/markdown"; title="Full LLM context"
  Vary: Accept

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-route Link header pointing at this page's matching `.md` endpoint.
# Cloudflare Pages and Netlify combine matching `_headers` rules, so the
# default rule above and the per-route rule below both apply.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------

